A||N||18||DaRkLoRD||Building a new file server, need opinions on what mobo, cpu, etc to use..||||3||
Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||12-11-2001||10:24 AM||||I want to do a "budget" file server.. cheap case (under $50), a decent but inexpensive motherboard, fairly fast cpu but less than 1ghz (read: cheapest cpu I can find...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) and about 256mb of ram.<P>Already have a ton of monitors, keyboards etc. kicking around, and the case will come with a power supply usually.. not worried about the hard drive, I already know what I need for that.. just a temporary thing anyway, because I intend to eventually make a raid setup for it. no cash for that now though, I'd need two 40 gig drives..<BR>In regards to the mobo & cpu, I'm partial to Intel, but AMDs are ok too, I'm not looking to overclock this.<BR>As for the case, an Antec would be cool, but a bit out of my price range for this project. (US$120 is what I've recently seen them for)<P>So.. your mission, should you choose to accept it.. (only 10am and I'm bored at work already) is to recommend what's the best system I could put together for under CDN $350, excluding the case, HD, and all external peripherals. (needs a cheap vidcard and NIC too..) I'm willing to go for lower speed CPUs if I can get a dual CPU setup, just cause they're cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'll probably run Win2k on this, not sure how much of an issue that'd be when deciding on components.<P>thanks!!<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000001||Formula88||12-11-2001||10:36 AM||||For those specs, just hit <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pricewatch.com</A>  and pick up one of their PC Kits.  Add your own HDD, video, etc. and you've got a file server for under $500 (less for &lt;1GHz CPU's).  <P>With the limitations you placed ($50 case, cheap mobo, etc) you're really only getting a PC that will be used as a file server.  For an actual server, you need a mobo that supports ECC memory, higher output powersupplies, etc.  An actual "server" isn't cheap.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||12-11-2001||11:23 AM||||My home lan is running off a 366 Celeron with a 20 gig drive. I run windows 2000 server and ISA firewall server to proxy to the internet. My workstations are all over 1ghz AMD's and there is no "lag" from the server even with a 100mbit network. I'm sure the transfers would be quicker with a faster server but it's plenty fast for my use.<P>Use 10/100 Network cards and hub/routers in your network. 10 is ok but it really cooks at 100 full duplex||||reg||14
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||12-11-2001||12:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>For those specs, just hit <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pricewatch.com</A>  and pick up one of their PC Kits.  Add your own HDD, video, etc. and you've got a file server for under $500 (less for &lt;1GHz CPU's).  <P>With the limitations you placed ($50 case, cheap mobo, etc) you're really only getting a PC that will be used as a file server.  For an actual server, you need a mobo that supports ECC memory, higher output powersupplies, etc.  An actual "server" isn't cheap.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, that's all I want.. just a regular PC that will be used to store files accessed over a network.. The one thing I don't really like about buying "kit" or barebones systems, is the cases are often.. well.. ugly. I want one that I can paint (yeah yeah.. I know.. but some of them look cool...) and add some extra fans to.. (I have some 4.5" case fans that are left over from my liquid cooled computer project.. they're loud, but they move enough air to blow themselves over..... they're heavy too.)<P>now, if I could find a place that woule let me pick a case, and then they build the barebones system in that (and adjust the price accordingly for the case..) then that'd be perfect.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||12-11-2001||02:18 PM||||Get a pile of old 486's and make a Linux cluster!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Could be a pretty cool project that would give you some background on doing it with better hardware (i.e. at work). I bought a whole stack of PC's at auction for $5 that included around 25 IBM 486's, a couple 386's and 2 PowerMac 6100's.<P>Just c'mon over and I'll donate a couple  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||12-11-2001||02:23 PM||||yeah, that would be cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>not quite what I'm looking for, but I definitely would build a cluster if I had the time and money.. mmm... liquid cooled rackmount beowulf cluster...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>add in a few gigs of ram... and that thing would be processing seti data faster than it could download it.. well, maybe not.. but it'd still be fun. I bet it'd be able to render 3D scenes pretty fast too, takes forever on my system.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000006||frontal lobe||12-11-2001||02:30 PM||||Mobo:  ECS K7S5A for about $65 shipped.  Has onboard LAN and sound<BR>CPU:  Athlon 1.4gHz at Thompson Computer Warehouse for about $106 shipped.<BR>RAM:  256mb DDR ram from Crucial for &lt;$50 shipped.<BR>Video:  Just get any old Diamond Viper 550 equivalent nvidia card used off a computer forum for $15 shipped.<P>That comes in at $235 U.S.  What's that convert to in Canadian dollars?<P>And you have a stable, fast system that you have nothing to be ashamed of.<P>Oops-add $15-20 for a cpu fan in there.  Or if you dropped down to a 1ghz athlon that would save you the $20 that you could spend on the fan.<P>Still-mighty fast for the price.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||12-11-2001||03:49 PM||||Frontal Lobe: that's the kind of info I've been looking for..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>need... more...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000008||84Bill||12-11-2001||04:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>Mobo:  ECS K7S5A for about $65 shipped.  Has onboard LAN and sound<BR>CPU:  Athlon 1.4gHz at Thompson Computer Warehouse for about $106 shipped.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow man where this place? They have a website?||||reg||1
Z||000009||frontal lobe||12-11-2001||04:23 PM||||Here is the way I have had good success in getting good prices and service by ordering over the internet:<P>1)  Go to <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com" TARGET=_blank>www.pricewatch.com</A>  to find the component I want.<P>2)  When I find a listing of vendors I may consider buying from, go to <A HREF="http://www.resellerratings.com" TARGET=_blank>www.resellerratings.com</A>  to make sure they are legitimate and good vendors.<P>3)  Go to <A HREF="http://www.anandtech.com" TARGET=_blank>www.anandtech.com</A>  to the "for sale" forum to pick up excellent condition used parts for cheap.  (I also LOVE the "hot deals" forum-have saved big bucks there, too.)<P>I have saved literally hundreds of dollars doing this.  84bill, if you need more instructions, let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||12-11-2001||11:10 PM||||For a dedicated Server, get two slower CPUs instead of one faster one.  EG dual 500 P3 will spank a single 800.<P>Ebay is a good place to find used dual p3 mobos with CPUs, SCSI, video, and LAN, etc onboard already!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||8Ball||12-12-2001||12:47 AM||||Ok get a Gigabyte MB<BR>And AMD Duron 800<BR>512 megs of ram in Dimms, They are cheap!!<BR>And get a cyclone dual or another HIGH END Cooling system. Aopen Case. The MB Should be Capable of 1.4 Ghz<P>This can ALL be done for around $400 and you can overclock to 1.2-1.4 easy so long as you keep it cool.<P>E.B.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||12-12-2001||10:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>For a dedicated Server, get two slower CPUs instead of one faster one.  EG dual 500 P3 will spank a single 800.<P>Ebay is a good place to find used dual p3 mobos with CPUs, SCSI, video, and LAN, etc onboard already!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>True, but that's only if you're running multi-threaded processes.  Otherwise the 2nd CPU is just taking up space.  Depends mostly on the OS.  Just an FYI, for anyone who wasn't familiar with multiple CPU systems.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||DaRkLoRD||12-12-2001||10:39 AM||||Anyone know if SETI@Home supports SMP under Win2k?<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000014||DaRkLoRD||12-12-2001||10:46 AM||||I want this thing to not only store some of my files, but also be able to run seti, render 3D images (freeing up resources on my workstation) etc.<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000015||Adrift||12-12-2001||10:47 AM||||You don't need a fast processor to have a good server... I hosted a few websites which together recieved about 50,000 uniques a day on a 233 PII with 256mb ram and a 8 gig hard drive (That's maybe a $100 CAN Machine Used?) - Never reached 80% capacity<P>But then again, I was running a unix clone (OpenBSD), not Windows 98/2000/NT, the devils gift to mankind (Still can't get over how slow they all are!)||||reg||1
Z||000016||DaRkLoRD||12-12-2001||10:52 AM||||Adrift, This would be storing about 20-40 gigs of mp3s, movies and other files (software install files, and my daily backups of work data) accessed thru a 10/100 network by my workstation and my sister's workstation.. which I have to finish rebuilding.. (P2-400, my old setup.. I gutted it for my P3 project, the P2 needs a new hard drive, sound, and some cables..)<P>I need something that will do this, and still be able to run memory/cpu intensive processes in the background, like rendering a complex 3D scene.. without casusing any lag in my mp3 playback.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000017||Blacktree||12-14-2001||03:23 AM||||Hmmm... so you wanna do file serving AND number crunching? Chances are that you'll want to go with a single processor (to save cash) in the 800MHz-1GHz range. Athlons/Durons are cheaper than Pentium3's, so you'll probably want to find a cheap yet stable AMD motherboard. Something with an AMD chipset will be more stable and perform better than one with a Via chipset.<P>If you check out <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com" TARGET=_blank>www.pricewatch.com</A>  , you'll find the Epox EP-8K7A board for about US $80 (US $79 from <A HREF="http://www.newegg.com" TARGET=_blank>www.newegg.com</A>  ). It has the AMD761 chipset (ATA100, AGP4X, DDR RAM, onboard audio, etc) and is regarded as a very stable motherboard. <P>Drop in an Athlon 1GHz (US $70 <A HREF="http://www.newegg.com" TARGET=_blank>www.newegg.com</A>  ) and 256MB of PC2100 DDR memory (US $56 from <A HREF="http://www.newegg.com" TARGET=_blank>www.newegg.com</A>  ) and you have yourself a pretty fast setup for about US $210 + shipping. <P>You can find TNT2 vid cards for about US $25-30 on Pricewatch. Oh, and don't forget to buy a heatsink/fan for that Athlon.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>You might also want to try out the Agora forum at <A HREF="http://arstechnica.infopop.net" TARGET=_blank>Ars Technica</A>. I've been a member of the Ars Technica forums for over 2 years and can honestly say it's a great place to get cheap prices on used computer hardware.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A><p>[This message has been edited by Blacktree (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Formula88||12-14-2001||10:56 AM||||Well, for the file server, you could get away with a used PC no problem.  With the number crunching, you're gonna have to figure out what it'll take to do that the way you want.  You may be better off doing the number crunching on your workstation and use an inexpensive file server.  I've used an IBM PC350 (Pentium 200) for a file server without problem.  I've even been able to use an old 486 as a simple file and printer server.  If you also want to use this server as a Firewall for a DSL or cable modem, then that's gonna up the requirements some more, or it'll be a bottleneck for your internet connection.<P>In my house, I use the P200 as a file server (running NT4, but I'm gonna switch to Linux) and any number crunching, etc. I do on my PIII 800 workstation.  I can run UT with SETI running in the backround without lag.||||reg||1
